{ White captures - if white takes black captures back with the queen. } ( 3. e5
{ If white pushes with e5, black should not play e6 because then the bishop on c8 is absolutely terrible, worse than a French bishop since there's no c5 square. Instead play Bf5 first. }
3... Bf5
{ Black plays Bf5 before e6, giving the bishop a good square. This is the right move order to avoid locking in the bishop. }
4. Nf3 { White develops the knight. } 4... e6
{ Now black can play e6, with the bishop already developed. } 5. Bd3
{ White plays Bd3 defending the e5 pawn - black should not take it right away. }
5... Nge7
{ Black develops the knight, not taking on d3 to avoid letting white develop for free. }
6. O-O { White castles kingside. } 6... Qd7
{ Black plays Qd7, eyeing the kingside. Black is looking to castle queenside with a rook on g8 eyeing g1. The engine gives black the advantage here. }
7. c3 { White plays c3. } 7... f6
