{ One option for black - if you're an e4/e5 player you can play e5 here. Nothing wrong with it, the position now looks like a standard e4/e5 opening with Nf3 Nc6. White may go into Ruy Lopez, Italian, Scotch, etc. The engine considers this the most sound move. }
( 2... d6
{ The Williams variation - a popular choice among Nimzowitsch Defense players. Against d4 black keeps developing, against d5 the knight centralizes, and against e5 black can capture and look to play Ng4 threatening Nxf2 winning the rook. }
3. d4 { White advances in the center. } 3... Nf6 { Black develops the knight. }
4. e5 { White pushes the pawn forward. } 4... dxe5
{ Black captures, opening up the d-file. } 5. dxe5
{ White recaptures with the pawn. } 5... Qxd1+
{ Black captures the queen with check. } 6. Kxd1
{ White is forced to recapture with the king. } 6... Ng4
{ Key idea - black plays Ng4. Now there are two attackers on e5, but the biggest threat is Nxf2 taking the rook. }
7. Ke1 { White moves the king to stop the threat. } 7... Ngxe5
{ Black captures on e5 and is up a pawn with a great position. } ) ( 2... f5
{ The Colorado Counter Gambit - a very fun chess opening for black. Black puts pressure on e4. }
3. exf5 { The best move for white is to take, which is what's usually seen. }
3... d5 { Black plays d5, the main response in the Colorado Counter Gambit. }
4. g4
{ If white tries g4 to hold the pawn, black is better. Black has the strong response h5 putting pressure on the g4 pawn. If white pushes g5, f5 is weak and black is at even material with white's kingside overextended. If white captures on h5 it stays very weak. If h3, white loses the rook and it's only move 6 with a black rook on h1. }
